#---
# purpose: "a receive clause that binds a tuple field, a list cell with its head and tail, and the fields of a binary reads every variable out of the roots the send gifted, whether the message was waiting in the mailbox or the receiver was parked"
# kind: run
#---
#
# One clause takes a message apart: `n` is a tuple field, `items` is a list cell
# whose head and tail are bound too, and the binary is split into `len`,
# `payload` and `more`. Of those variables only the cell overlaps another, and
# the head and tail are read out of the cell the receiver holds. The program
# receives the message twice: once found in the mailbox, once sent to a parked
# receiver, which is a gift. Both print the same shape.
def take() do
  receive do
    {:frame, n, [_, head | rest] = items, <<len, payload::binary-size(len), more::binary>>} ->
      {n, head, rest, items, len, payload, more}
  after
    60000 -> :timeout
  end
end

def sender(parent) do
  receive do
    :go -> send(parent, {:frame, 7, [10, 20, 30, 40], <<3, 65, 66, 67, 68, 69>>})
  end
end

def main() do
  send(self(), {:frame, 1, [1, 2, 3], <<1, 88, 89>>})
  dbg(take())

  parent = self()
  child = spawn(fn () -> sender(parent) end)
  send(child, :go)
  dbg(take())
end
